@@s001 Triage: recognising the paradigm
@@s002 Triage: recognising the paradigm
@@s003 How to recognise the family of a problem never seen before, in seven observations, with a decision tree and the signals of the text.
@@s004 The seven observations
@@s005 On each problem make these observations in order. The first three are almost always enough; the others separate greedy, DP and NP-hard.
@@s006 <b>What comes out?</b> A yes/no, a number, a set, a sequence. If a <i>count</i> comes out ("as many as possible", "fewest") or a <i>sum of values</i> ("maximise total pages"), flag it: that is the difference between greedy and DP.
@@s007 <b>What structure ties the objects together?</b> None (independent list) → greedy, knapsack or Partition. A linear order (sequence, string, intervals, pyramid) → DP. An explicit or hidden graph (grid, "infects", "prerequisite") → traversal, flow or NP-hard. Two sets to connect → flow.
@@s008 <b>If there is a graph, what does it ask about paths?</b> Does one exist? The shortest? Which nodes can I reach? → traversal (BFS, DFS, Dijkstra). More disjoint paths, or the minimum to destroy to separate → flow (Menger). A path that touches all the nodes, or a node cover → NP-hard, unless the graph is a tree or a DAG.
@@s009 <b>Are there objects to assign with a limit?</b> "each … exactly one", "at most c(i)", "cannot be used twice". Yes → flow: source → side A → side B → sink.
@@s010 <b>Is there a single numerical budget?</b> You maximise the number of objects → greedy, take the cheapest. You maximise a sum of values → DP knapsack.
@@s011 <b>Try the most natural greedy on 3–4 objects.</b> You find a counterexample → it is not greedy: it is DP or NP-hard. You do not find one and can say why it holds → greedy.
@@s012 <b>Fix a parameter at an extreme value</b> (t = 0, k = 2, r = n, all nodes red): if it becomes Independent Set, Vertex Cover, Hamiltonian path, 3SAT or Set Cover, it is NP-hard, and you already have the reduction.
@@s013 Decision tree
@@s014 Read it from the top. On the left the structure of the input, on the right what the output measures or what it asks about the graph. DP, flow and NP-hard appear on several branches: it is a word of the text that chooses the branch.
@@s015 structure
@@s016 question 1
@@s017 question 2
@@s018 list of objects (numbers, costs, budget)
@@s019 count objects or take the k best → greedy
@@s020 sum of values within the budget → DP (knapsack)
@@s021 an order (sequence, string, DAG, tree, pyramid)
@@s022 optimum from prefixes, intervals, subtrees → DP
@@s023 two parts exactly equal → NP-hard
@@s024 two sets to pair with limits
@@s025 each assigned within the capacities → flow
@@s026 a graph (explicit or hidden)
@@s027 a path: does it exist? the shortest? → traversal
@@s028 disjoint paths or minimum cut → flow; path through all nodes → NP-hard
@@s029 Signals in the text
@@s030 phrase in the text
@@s031 paradigm
